In article <91077.201302CERLPDR@TECHNION.BITNET> CERLPDR@TECHNION.BITNET writes:
>>leonard@mimsy.umd.edu (Leonard Dickens) writes:
>>
>>What alignment do you favor?
>
>I've found chaotic to be the best.
>
Once you have a helm of opposite alignment, most of the differences
vanish.  At that point, the only real difference is what sorts of monsters
you can avoid tangling with.  I'm somewhat partial to lawfulness,
because a titan can be one of the nastiest things around, and they're
generally peaceful towards lawful characters.

If you don't have the helm yet, Chaotic is the easiest to play
for a couple of reasons:

  1) The gods accept sacrifices more readily, and the limited number
     of chaotic weapons makes Stormbringer easier to get (as David
     Rodriguez noted).

  2) You can pray and get help in Hell

>>It seems to me that the best alignment
>>to be is Lawful, for the following reasons:
>>1) Two of the best artifacts (Mjollnir, Excaliber) available from god.
>
> Really? I always thought Excaliber was highly overrated. In any case,
>if one really wants it, Excaliber can also be obtained somewhat more
>easily from a fountain.
>

Excalibur is indeed overrated, especially if you're running around
with Stormbringer.  If Mjollnir or Fire/Frost Brand is your weapon
of choice though, make an Excalibur at a fountain and keep it handy.
Wield it when fighting wraiths and vampires to prevent a level drain.

>
>>Chaotic characters *do* have a good
>>chance of getting Stormbringer from their god.
>
>  This is what tips the balance for me. There are simply too many
>lawful artifacts. Getting Mjollnir can take forever. (Although I
>have had games where the gods give me Grimtooth and Cleaver and then
>become stingy for a *long* while before finally bestowing
>Stormbringer.
>
If you ARE trying to get Mjollnir, be sure to first get a two-handed
sword and name it Orcrist and an elven dagger and name it Sting. That
will make sure that the gods don't reward your sacrifices with either
of these and speed up getting Mojo.


